From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:38:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: decoder mode: do not wait for the GPU with the
 BQL held

A frame converted on the GPU stays referenced until the GPU has read
it (dec_gpu_hold()), and at the third frame the main loop waited for
the oldest copy's fence, up to 200 ms, with the BQL held. Measured on
a Raspberry Pi 5 (2026-10-24, H.264 1080p60 in a WebView beside the
HMI, perf sched_switch on the main loop for 30 seconds): it waited
there 280 times, up to 48 ms, 24 times over 20 ms. Everything else
behind the BQL waited too, among them the sound card: DomA's audio
buffers were given back and taken in late, and DomD's ALSA output ran
dry.

virtio_media_gpu_poll() tells whether a copy is done without waiting,
from its fence fd (gpu-fence-fd=on). dec_gpu_hold() first lets go of
the frames whose copies are done, and with a fence fd a software codec
(H.264) holds the new frame beside the ones not done yet, up to 8,
instead of waiting; the next frame tries them again. The frames held
are buffers of the zero-copy pool, which grows by them (to 32 at most,
then the staging path as before). A hardware codec (HEVC, whose buffers
are v4l2request's, in CMA) and a copy without a fence fd still wait at
the third frame.

When every held frame goes (a new stream, a flush, the end), the done
ones go first and the rest are waited for; after one wait fails the
rest go without waiting, so the main loop is not held for 8 times
200 ms. A copy that fails, or gets no fence, keeps the previous copy's
fence, so a frame held for that copy still waits for it.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on this layer's virtio-media device]
